#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096323 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5$, calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} = 5g^4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 5g^{4} \\cdot (12x^{3} + 3x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 5(3x^{4} + x^{3})^{4}(12x^{3} + 3x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096324 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable.\n\nLet $u=1-x^2$. Then\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -2x\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\cdot (-2x) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}}$, calculate $f'(x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write $f(x)=(1-3x)^{-1/2}$. Let $u=1-3x$, so $du/dx=-3$. By the chain rule,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=-\\frac12u^{-3/2}\\frac{du}{dx}\\\\\n&=-\\frac12u^{-3/2}(-3)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe original function and this derivative are defined for $x<1/3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c720d59-e194-5266-b56a-c466a517a985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5x - 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095384} {:answer/id #uuid "f0193492-5011-5859-a30f-bbc8de469e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x - 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095385} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb017af-74fd-54ab-b3e7-3cf1930e878c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5x - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095386} {:answer/id #uuid "144cb047-1208-5ac8-84b5-438b8948026c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac1{2\\sqrt{(5x-1)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095387} {:answer/id #uuid "628b0cb7-1d47-5296-86cf-7c96168acd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac5{2\\sqrt{(5x-1)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "628b0cb7-1d47-5296-86cf-7c96168acd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac5{2\\sqrt{(5x-1)^3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095388} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efc3cd7-9913-56b8-a464-cc38a8cdd7e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095383}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "854ec833-4bc0-54f0-9210-c708a9342fef" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48730" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x - 1}}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Write $y=(5x-1)^{-1/2}$. For $x>1/5$, the chain rule gives\n$$y'=-\\frac12(5x-1)^{-3/2}\\cdot5=-\\frac5{2(5x-1)^{3/2}}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93d3a4bb-9046-5724-81a5-d6c8132b738b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+6x)^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095828} {:answer/id #uuid "da4b4e40-db69-587e-bc11-6cc7a4c06e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(4 + 6x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095829} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd52c05-d5cf-599c-9ad4-6f7e03723c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095830} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2fd34d-bfa7-516f-99c6-8073eadb8769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095831} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcfc72a-1932-5f07-a4d3-625ca2e743dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dcfc72a-1932-5f07-a4d3-625ca2e743dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4 + 6x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095832} :answer-field/id #uuid "42bed18e-2ea3-5815-9102-05a6b242e840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095827}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0bd56a8-5c99-5166-be8e-4185bfb62ed5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-666" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (4 + 6x)^{3}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The outer cube has derivative $3u^2$, and the inner function $u=4+6x$ has derivative $6$. Thus\n$$f'=3(4+6x)^2\\cdot6=18(4+6x)^2.$$"}]}
